<h2>Fifteen years from decree to decree</h2>
<p>The parties married on 10 March 2002; a son was born in December 2002. The wife&rsquo;s case was of a Rs 4 lakh dowry demand, physical and mental cruelty, verbal abuse, and her ouster from the matrimonial home on 17 May 2003. The husband denied everything, counter-alleged a Rs 10 lakh demand by her family, imputed on her character, and professed willingness to restore conjugal life. The Family Court dismissed her petition in 2011, finding neither cruelty nor desertion proved. Her first appeal was finally decided on 5 November 2026 — a reminder of how long matrimonial appeals can remain pending, and of why appellate courts now routinely attend to maintenance in the interim.</p>

<h2>The standard of proof did the work</h2>
<p>The Bench&rsquo;s starting point was the standard of proof: matrimonial cases are decided on preponderance of probabilities, not proof beyond reasonable doubt. On that footing, the Family Court&rsquo;s insistence on exactitude was misplaced. As the judgment records, the wife&rsquo;s &ldquo;inability to disclose the date was not fatal to the allegations of demand of dowry&rdquo;. What mattered was the consistency of her account across examination and cross-examination, and the support it drew from corroborating witnesses.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>The evidentiary lesson:</strong> consistency and corroboration outweigh calendar precision. A witness who cannot date every incident is not thereby disbelieved — particularly for conduct spread over months inside a household.</p>
</div>

<h2>Public abuse and character imputations</h2>
<p>Two strands of conduct carried the cruelty finding. The first was verbal abuse of the wife in public — humiliation before others being a recognised form of mental cruelty. The second was the husband&rsquo;s own litigation conduct: the imputations he cast on the wife&rsquo;s character. Since <em>Vijaykumar Ramchandra Bhate</em>, unproved aspersions of this kind in pleadings have themselves been treated as mental cruelty, and the Bench read the husband&rsquo;s allegations in that light. Taken together, the court held the conduct crossed the threshold of Section 13(1)(i-a).</p>

<h2>Where the wife&rsquo;s case failed — and why it did not matter</h2>
<p>The desertion ground under Section 13(1)(i-b) was rejected: the husband&rsquo;s professed desire to restore conjugal life meant animus deserendi could not be established against him. The Bench also declined the invitation to rest the decree on irretrievable breakdown, holding that it is not a statutory ground available to a High Court — a power the Supreme Court alone exercises under Article 142 of the Constitution. Neither limitation affected the result, because the cruelty finding independently sustained the decree: &ldquo;The marriage between the parties stands dissolved by a decree of divorce on the ground of cruelty.&rdquo;</p>

<h2>Takeaways</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For petitioners.</strong> Build the record on consistency: a coherent narrative, corroborating family witnesses, and contemporaneous material beat a list of dated incidents imperfectly remembered.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For respondents.</strong> Character imputations in a written statement are not cost-free. If they cannot be proved, they can become the cruelty that dissolves the marriage.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>On strategy.</strong> Pleading desertion alongside cruelty is common, but each ground has its own anatomy; a professed willingness to cohabit by the other side will usually defeat desertion while leaving cruelty untouched.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>In Delhi.</strong> The same principles govern Section 13 HMA petitions before Delhi&rsquo;s Family Courts, with first appeals heard by a Division Bench of the High Court of Delhi under Section 19 of the Family Courts Act, 1984.</div>
</div>
